# Sample 1 - HelloAAuth manual requests
# Start sample:
#   dotnet run --project samples/HelloAAuth -- interactive
# Get token and signing key in REPL:
#   token          → export AGENT_JWT='<paste>'
#   signing-key    → export SIGNING_KEY='<paste>'
# Generate signed headers:
#   dotnet run --project labs/scripts/AAuth.SignTool -- --jwt "$AGENT_JWT" --signing-key "$SIGNING_KEY" --method GET --url "https://localhost:3012/api/hello"
# NOTE: Signature-Input created timestamp is valid for ~60 seconds.
# Regenerate and resend immediately if request returns 401.

@apBase = https://localhost:3011
@resourceBase = https://localhost:3012
@agentJwt = {{$processEnv AGENT_JWT}}

### Metadata - Agent Provider
GET {{apBase}}/.well-known/aauth-agent.json

# VERIFY:
#   - 200 OK
#   - Body contains "jwks_uri" pointing to AP's JWKS endpoint
#   - Body contains "identifier" with the AP's AAuth identifier

### Metadata - Agent Provider JWKS
GET {{apBase}}/jwks

# VERIFY:
#   - 200 OK
#   - Body is a JWK Set with at least one key (the AP's root public key)
#   - Key "kid" matches the agent token's issuer key

### VALID signed call (expected 200)
# Paste values from signing script:
@sigKey = REPLACE_SIGNATURE_KEY
@sigInput = REPLACE_SIGNATURE_INPUT
@sig = REPLACE_SIGNATURE

GET {{resourceBase}}/api/hello
Signature-Key: {{sigKey}}
Signature-Input: {{sigInput}}
Signature: {{sig}}

# VERIFY:
#   - 200 OK
#   - Body JSON contains "agent" field matching the agent ID from the token
#   - This proves: agent token valid + HTTP signature verified + agent on allowlist

### UNSIGNED call (expected 401)
GET {{resourceBase}}/api/hello

# VERIFY:
#   - 401 Unauthorized
#   - No Signature/Signature-Input/Signature-Key headers → resource rejects immediately
#   - This proves: HTTP Message Signatures are required, not optional

### UNKNOWN/FORGED token call (expected 401 or 403)
# ⚠️ RE-SIGN REQUIRED: Generate fresh signature headers for this step.
# The headers from the "VALID" step above have likely expired (>60s).
#
# Simple forged-token recipe:
# 1) Copy the valid AGENT_JWT.
# 2) Change ONE character in the JWT signature part (the 3rd segment, after the second '.').
# 3) Use that tampered JWT as @unknownJwt, generate headers, and send (expect 401/403).
# Optional "bad signature" variant:
# - Keep @unknownJwt as-is, but change one character in @unknownSig before sending (expect 401).
@unknownJwt = REPLACE_UNKNOWN_JWT
@unknownSigKey = REPLACE_SIGNATURE_KEY
@unknownSigInput = REPLACE_SIGNATURE_INPUT
@unknownSig = REPLACE_SIGNATURE

GET {{resourceBase}}/api/hello
Signature-Key: {{unknownSigKey}}
Signature-Input: {{unknownSigInput}}
Signature: {{unknownSig}}

# VERIFY:
#   - 401 or 403
#   - 401 = JWT signature invalid (tampered token) or HTTP signature mismatch
#   - 403 = valid JWT but agent not on allowlist
#   - This proves: the resource validates the full trust chain, not just header presence
